specs/hal-engine-spec-anchor-drift/spec.md:12:Any relative path with a file extension followed by `#L<n>`, in any spec under `specs/<slug>/spec.md` plus `.specify/spec.md`. A link whose fragment is not a line number is not an anchor and is left alone, however much the file around it moves ([validated by: leaves a link whose fragment is not a line number untouched](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L506)). Test files are the common case, but nothing restricts it to them: a citation into a workflow, a config or a script is repointed the same way ([validated by: repoints docs, README and script citations alike](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L454), [root config](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L476), [workflow and manifest](../../scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.test.ts#L490)).
specs/hal-engine-doc-gates/spec.md:62:Two steps of that job are deliberately outside the script: the spec anchor check and the changelog check both diff against `origin/main`, and neither behaves the same on a checkout as it does against a pull request's base.
specs/hal-engine-doc-gates/spec.md:66:`npm run docs:check-paths` resolves every backticked repository path written in a document - `src/`, `scripts/`, `example/`, `smoke/`, `docs/`, `specs/`, `adrs/`, `dist/`, `.github/` and `.specify/`. A path that legitimately does not resolve is named in the script's own allowlist with the reason, because an exception has to be written down to be one; narrowing the pattern instead would hide every other path behind the same prefix. A path in prose is a citation, and a citation nobody resolves rots in silence: the file moves, the sentence naming it stays, and the next reader follows it to nothing.
specs/hal-engine-doc-gates/spec.md:106:`npm run spec:names` holds every `([validated by: <name>](path#Lnn))` citation to the declaration it names, and `spec:names:fix` repoints the line from the name.
